WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.480
The same synapse can be turned up or turned down for hours.

2
00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:08.820
What decides which way it goes?

3
00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:15.510
Start with up. In the standard model, the large, fast calcium rise after a tetanus

4
00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:17.930
recruits a kinase, CaMKII.

5
00:00:18.933 --> 00:00:21.913
This kinase can stay active after calcium falls.

6
00:00:22.673 --> 00:00:26.093
So a brief calcium signal can leave a change that outlasts it.

7
00:00:26.983 --> 00:00:30.543
Expression includes more AMPA receptors at the synapse.

8
00:00:31.383 --> 00:00:37.063
In 2000, Hayashi, Malinow and colleagues saw tagged AMPA receptors driven into

9
00:00:37.063 --> 00:00:38.063
synapses.

10
00:00:38.633 --> 00:00:42.853
More AMPA receptors mean a bigger response to the same glutamate.

11
00:00:43.983 --> 00:00:48.063
LTP that lasts many hours often needs new protein synthesis.

12
00:00:49.183 --> 00:00:55.843
Now down. In 1992, Serena Dudek and Mark Bear gave the same input a long, slow

13
00:00:55.843 --> 00:01:00.083
train. It was 900 pulses, at 1 to 3 Hz.

14
00:01:01.067 --> 00:01:05.447
The response fell and stayed down for more than an hour, only at the conditioned

15
00:01:05.447 --> 00:01:09.047
input. This is long-term depression, or LTD.

16
00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:13.800
Now repeat the slow train with the NMDA blocker APV.

17
00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:15.960
Does LTD develop?

18
00:01:22.033 --> 00:01:25.973
No. NMDA antagonists prevented it.

19
00:01:26.693 --> 00:01:30.133
The same receptor that gates LTP is needed for its opposite.

20
00:01:31.233 --> 00:01:32.893
So what decides the direction?

21
00:01:33.773 --> 00:01:37.273
In the standard model, how much calcium comes through, and how fast.

22
00:01:38.333 --> 00:01:43.493
A modest, prolonged rise preferentially recruits protein phosphatases, rather than

23
00:01:43.493 --> 00:01:44.513
CaMKII.

24
00:01:45.483 --> 00:01:50.763
The model's reason: the phosphatase pathway responds to smaller Ca2+/calmodulin

25
00:01:50.763 --> 00:01:53.083
signals than CaMKII does.

26
00:01:54.067 --> 00:01:57.327
So a small, long signal tips toward removing receptors.

27
00:01:58.307 --> 00:02:00.667
A large, fast one tips toward inserting them.

28
00:02:01.667 --> 00:02:07.687
In 1993, Mulkey, Herron and Malenka found that phosphatase inhibitors blocked LTD

29
00:02:07.687 --> 00:02:09.827
induction in rat CA1.

30
00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:16.510
Expression of LTD includes removing AMPA receptors by endocytosis.

31
00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:21.030
Keep the hedge. This calcium rule is a model.

32
00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:26.710
No single calcium threshold between LTD and LTP is established.

33
00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:31.670
Whether LTD needs calcium flow through the receptor is still debated.

34
00:02:32.733 --> 00:02:37.173
The takeaway: in the standard model, a synapse turns up or down with how much

35
00:02:37.173 --> 00:02:39.113
calcium enters, and how fast.
